What is Sliding Window?

Sliding window = a moving range inside an array/string
Instead of checking all substrings again and again,
you: keep a window [left … right]
expand it, shrink it only when rule breaks

Think of it like:
“I am looking at a part of the string.
I move forward without going backward.”

🔥 Why Sliding Window exists
Brute force substring:
O(n²) or O(n³) -> slow
Sliding window:
O(n) -> one pass

🧠 The CORE IDEA (memorize this)
left = 0
for right from 0 → n-1:
    add s[right] to window

    while window is invalid:
        remove s[left]
        left++

    update answer using window size


That’s it.
Every sliding window problem fits this shape.

🧩 When do you use Sliding Window?
If the question has:
substring / subarray
longest / shortest
at most / at least / exactly
without repeating
sum / count / frequency

👉 Sliding Window

🧱 Sliding Window has ONLY 2 TYPES

TYPE 1️⃣ Fixed Window Size
Window size = constant k
Examples:
max sum subarray of size k
average of window k
substrings of length k

Pattern:
int left = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
    // add right element

    if (right - left + 1 == k) {
        // process window
        // remove left
        left++;
    }
}

TYPE 2️⃣ Variable Window Size (MOST IMPORTANT)
Window grows & shrinks based on condition
Examples:
longest substring without repeating characters
minimum window substring
subarray sum ≤ k

🧠 Visual example (VERY IMPORTANT)
String: "abcabcbb"
Goal: longest substring without repeating chars

We maintain:
left → start of window
right → end of window
Set → what's inside window

Step 1: [a]
Step 2: [a b]
Step 3: [a b c]
Step 4: 'a' repeats → shrink from left
        [b c a]

👉 Window slides forward, never backward.

🧠 The 4 Questions you MUST ask yourself

When you see substring / array question, ask:

Q1: Window size fixed or variable?
fixed → type 1
depends on condition → type 2

Q2: What makes window INVALID?
Examples:
duplicate character
sum > k
missing required character

Q3: What data structure do I need?
duplicate → Set or Map
frequency → int[26] or Map

Q4: What answer do I update?
max length?
min length?
count?

🧪 Example 1: Longest substring without repeating

Rule broken when:
duplicate character enters window

Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
int left = 0, max = 0;

for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    while (set.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
        set.remove(s.charAt(left));// left means count/index present at left for that position like, if left = 2, s.charAt(2)
        left++;
    }
    set.add(s.charAt(right));
    max = Math.max(max, right - left + 1);
}

🧪 Example 2: Max sum subarray of size k

Rule:
window size must be k

int sum = 0, maxSum = 0;
int left = 0;

for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];

    if (right - left + 1 == k) {
        maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, sum);
        sum -= arr[left];
        left++;
    }
}

❌ What Sliding Window is NOT
NOT recursion
NOT nested loops
NOT brute force
NOT backtracking

🧠 Mental Shortcut (SUPER IMPORTANT)
Whenever you see:
substring / subarray
Immediately say in your head:
“Can I use sliding window?”
90% of time → yes.

📌 One-line memory rule
Expand with right, shrink with left, never go backward.

