2️⃣ THE UNIVERSAL SLIDING WINDOW TEMPLATE (memorize)
This works for array + string problems.

int left = 0;
DataStructure window; // Set / Map / array
Answer ans = initial;

for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
    // 1) add right element
    add(s[right]);

    // 2) shrink until window becomes valid
    while (window is invalid) {
        remove(s[left]);
        left++;
    }

    // 3) update answer
    ans = update(ans, right - left + 1);
}

🧠 Translation in simple English
Expand → Check → Shrink → Update → Repeat

3️⃣ How to DECIDE sliding window logic (mental checklist)
When you see a question, answer these 4 questions on paper or in head:
Q1️⃣ Is it substring / subarray?
If yes → sliding window possible

Q2️⃣ Is window size fixed or variable?
“size k” → fixed window
“longest / smallest / without / at most” → variable window

Q3️⃣ What breaks the rule?
Examples:
duplicate character
sum > k
frequency mismatch
missing character

Q4️⃣ What DS do I need?
Condition	        DS
duplicate check	    Set
last index	        Map
frequency	        int[26] / Map
sum	                int

4️⃣ Fixed vs Variable window (very clear)
🔹 Fixed window example
Max sum of subarray of size k
int sum = 0, left = 0, max = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < arr.length; right++) {
    sum += arr[right];
    if (right - left + 1 == k) {
        max = Math.max(max, sum);
        sum -= arr[left];
        left++;
    }
}

🔹 Variable window example
Longest substring without repeating
Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
int left = 0, max = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
    while (set.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
        set.remove(s.charAt(left));
        left++;
    }
    set.add(s.charAt(right));
    max = Math.max(max, right - left + 1);
}

